cartridge "POOL/Joseph T. Woyton/1985"

' POOL
' A ColecoVision game based on "Pool", COMPUTE!'s Gazette April 1985
' (Commodore 64 machine language, POOL/64 loading PL at $C000; a separate
' VIC-20 BASIC version on the same disk). No author is named on the disk.
' Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by
' Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: a cue ball and six coloured balls racked in a triangle. Move
' the ring (the aim) with the stick: the cue ball is sent towards the
' ring, and the farther the ring, the harder the shot. Press a button to
' shoot. Sink every ball. One player: the fewer shots, the better the
' rank (PRO, POOL SHARK, AMATEUR, NOVICE). Two players: you shoot again
' after sinking a ball; whoever sinks more balls wins.
'
' From the C64 machine code (orig/pl.dis):
' - six object balls in a triangle (sprites 2-7, rack at $CC96), the cue
'   ball on the head spot; the aim is a pointer sprite moved inside a box
'   of 80 pixels round the cue ball ($C12A); the shot's speed is the
'   pointer's offset from the cue ball, per axis ($C17E)
' - fixed-point motion (8.8, $6500/$6520), friction that halves the speed
'   of every ball ($CD41) until all are below 6/256 pixel a step ($C25D)
' - balls collide when their centres are within 8 pixels; the collision
'   exchanges the speed along the line between the centres, by a rotation
'   table ($CDC7, $C6A8-$C918): an elastic collision of equal balls
' - the rails bounce; a ball that reaches a rail at a corner or at the
'   middle of a long rail drops into the pocket ($C290, $C2B5)
' - a sunk ball goes to the shooter's side; with the cue ball sunk
'   (scratch) or with no ball touched in the shot ($C285) it is a foul:
'   one of the shooter's balls comes back to the table, the next shot is
'   taken with the cue ball in hand behind the head string, shooting away
'   from it ($C30A, $C36E)
' - two players: the turn passes when no ball was sunk or after a foul
'   (CCF8); the one with more balls wins, or IT'S A TIE (3-3)
' - one player counts shots: under 9 PRO, 9-11 POOL SHARK, 12-20
'   AMATEUR, 21 or more NOVICE ($CE1B)
'
' Liberties: the table is drawn sideways (the C64's stood upright) and
' larger: 184 x 120 pixels of travel against 92 x 104, so it is longer
' than wide like a real table; motion is computed once a frame in 1/256
' pixel (the C64 stepped many times a frame); its friction (halving the
' speed every 1024 steps, about 2.4 seconds measured by counting the
' loop's cycles) becomes a smooth 1/32 every third frame, keeping the
' direction, and a ball stops below 40/256 pixel a frame (the C64's
' 6/256 a step at about 7 steps a frame); the fastest shot is 3 pixels a
' frame (the C64's 80/256 a step, about 2.2 pixels a frame), and the aim
' ring goes 48 pixels from the cue ball (the C64's 80, on a smaller
' table); collisions use the true line between the centres (the C64
' rounded it by its table) and a circle of 8 pixels (the C64's was a
' square); pockets are wider at the corners (8 pixels along each rail)
' and 12 pixels at the sides, so a player can aim at them on a TV; after
' a foul the returned ball goes to the foot spot (the C64 put it in the
' middle) and slides along until it is clear; a power bar; the sunk balls
' shown in trays; no keypad (joystick menus); sounds and the title tune
' "Corner Pocket" are new.
'
' Controls: the stick moves the aim ring (faster after a moment), either
' button shoots. With the cue ball in hand, the stick moves the cue ball
' behind the head string and a button sets it down. Two players share
' controller 1 or use 1 and 2 by turns (either works for the shooter).
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off.

const TFelt = 128
const THead = 129
const TSpot = 130
const TRailt = 131
